// Title: Potential Registry Persistence Attempt Via DbgManagedDebugger
// Sigma ID: 9827ae57-3802-418f-994b-d5ecf5cd974b
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: frack113
// Date: 2022-08-07 | Modified: 2023-08-17
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, persistence, execution, stealth, T1574
// Description: Detects the addition of the "Debugger" value to the "DbgManagedDebugger" key in order to achieve
//   persistence. Which will get invoked when an application crashes
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of the key to setup a debugger. Which is often the case on developers machines
// References:
//   - https://www.hexacorn.com/blog/2013/09/19/beyond-good-ol-run-key-part-4/
//   - https://github.com/last-byte/PersistenceSniper
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_dbgmanageddebugger_persistence.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey endswith "\\Microsoft\\.NETFramework\\DbgManagedDebugger" and (not(RegistryValueData =~ "\"C:\\Windows\\system32\\vsjitdebugger.exe\" PID %d APPDOM %d EXTEXT \"%s\" EVTHDL %d"))
